{"id":"p213AsY9ekQ2Zy5c","meta":{"instanceId":"c2650793f644091dc80fb900fe63448ad1f4b774008de9608064d67294f8307c"},"name":"Score incident severity with Jev Classification, Google Sheets, Gmail, Telegram, and gpt-6-luna","tags":[],"nodes":[{"id":"df919e5a-ba8a-4db1-ba2c-7c6b63a45e7a","name":"Sticky Note","type":"n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote","position":[-480,-416],"parameters":{"width":460,"height":1040,"content":"## Production incident severity scoring\n\n### How it works\n\nAn incident report arrives on the Incident Intake webhook. Normalize Incident stamps an id and a time, then Incident Report Usable? requires a service name and an impact text, so an unusable report is marked and emailed back instead of scored.\n\nJev Score Incident Severity returns level, score, confidence and needsReview. Route By Confidence And Severity splits that into five lanes: SEV1 declares and pages the commander, SEV2 pages the on call engineer, SEV3 opens a ticket, SEV4 waits in the backlog, and a low confidence score is held for the commander.\n\nEach lane appends to the Incident Severity tab before it notifies anyone, and SEV1 sends an AI drafted stakeholder email after the page. A monthly cron reads that tab and emails a counted trend brief.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- [ ] Point alerting or your status page at the Incident Intake webhook (POST).\n- [ ] Connect Jev, Google Sheets, Gmail and Telegram credentials.\n- [ ] Set owners, SLA hours and recipients to your rota.\n- [ ] Confirm the monthly trend cron and activate the workflow.\n\n### Customization\n\n- Adjust the severity bands and the confidence threshold on the scoring node.\n- Add a lane for your own escalation path, or swap the pager for Slack."},"typeVersion":1},{"id":"72ac0681-1e7c-4e0b-a59e-9e76dec7170c","name":"Incident Intake","type":"n8n-nodes-base.webhook","position":[0,0],"webhookId":"8671b31b-f567-485a-bbfd-1a27b99d2e86","parameters":{"path":"jev-c2-incident-intake","options":{},"httpMethod":"POST"},"typeVersion":2},{"id":